Microsoft Learn: Configure Azure Virtual Network Peering

Platform: Microsoft Learn
Module: Configure Azure Virtual Network Peering
Track: AZ-104 Microsoft Azure Administrator
Difficulty: Intermediate
Date: 2026

Objective

Learn how to configure Azure Virtual Network (VNet) Peering to enable secure communication between virtual networks, implement gateway transit, manage connectivity between Azure environments, and extend network routing using service chaining and user-defined routes.

This module focused on connecting Azure Virtual Networks without requiring VPNs, understanding peering configurations, enabling gateway transit, and building scalable network architectures across Azure environments.

Step 1: Understanding Azure Virtual Network Peering

The module introduced Azure Virtual Network Peering as a method for connecting Azure Virtual Networks directly through Microsoft's private backbone network.

Virtual Network Peering allows resources in separate VNets to communicate as if they were on the same network.

Benefits of VNet Peering:
Low latency communication
High bandwidth connectivity
Private network communication
Simplified network architecture
No public internet exposure
Example:
VNet-A
10.1.0.0/16

       ↔

VNet-B
10.2.0.0/16

Resources in both networks can communicate securely after peering is established.

The module emphasized that peering uses Microsoft's private network infrastructure rather than the public internet.

Step 2: Determining Azure Virtual Network Peering Uses

The training covered common use cases for VNet Peering.

Common scenarios:
Connecting production and development environments
Separating application tiers
