The module demonstrated how peering supports enterprise cloud networking designs.

Step 3: Understanding Peering Requirements

A major focus of the module was understanding peering prerequisites.

Requirements:
VNets must use non-overlapping IP address spaces
Peering must be configured between VNets
Appropriate permissions are required
Example:

Valid:

VNet-A
10.1.0.0/16

VNet-B
10.2.0.0/16

Invalid:

VNet-A
10.1.0.0/16

VNet-B
10.1.0.0/16
Key lessons learned:
Overlapping address spaces prevent peering
Proper IP planning is critical
Network architecture should be designed before deployment
Step 4: Understanding Gateway Transit

The training introduced Gateway Transit.

Gateway Transit allows one VNet to share its VPN Gateway with other peered VNets.

Example:
On-Premises Network
        │
    VPN Gateway
        │
      Hub VNet
      /      \
     /        \
Spoke-A     Spoke-B
Benefits:
Reduced cost
Centralized connectivity
Simplified management
Key concepts learned:
Only one gateway is required
Spoke networks can use the shared gateway
Commonly used in hub-and-spoke architectures

The module emphasized Gateway Transit as an important enterprise networking feature.

