Step 6: Creating Virtual Network Peering

The training demonstrated how to create VNet Peering connections.

Administrative tasks practiced:
Selecting source VNet
Selecting target VNet
Configuring peering settings
Enabling virtual network access
Configuring gateway options
Peering options:
Setting	Purpose
Allow Virtual Network Access	Enable communication between VNets
Allow Forwarded Traffic	Permit forwarded network traffic
Allow Gateway Transit	Share VPN gateway
Use Remote Gateways	Consume shared gateway
Key lessons learned:
Peering is configured at both VNets
Connectivity settings determine traffic behavior
Gateway settings affect hybrid connectivity
Step 7: Extending Peering with User-Defined Routes

The module introduced User-Defined Routes (UDRs).

User-Defined Routes allow administrators to override Azure's default routing behavior.

Common use cases:
Directing traffic through Azure Firewall
Sending traffic through network virtual appliances
Implementing custom network paths
Benefits:
Greater routing control
Enhanced security
Traffic inspection
Key concepts learned:
Azure automatically creates system routes
UDRs override default routes
Route tables can be associated with subnets

The module demonstrated advanced network traffic management techniques.

Step 8: Understanding Service Chaining

A major focus of the module was Service Chaining.

Service Chaining allows network traffic to flow through virtual appliances before reaching its destination.

Example:
Client
   ↓
Azure Firewall
   ↓
Application Server
Benefits:
Security inspection
Traffic filtering
Centralized policy enforcement
Common virtual appliances:
Firewalls
Intrusion Detection Systems
Network Monitoring Solutions

The module emphasized Service Chaining as a key enterprise security feature.

